# @boundary runs
#
# BUG-234 REGRESSION GUARD. Authored 2026-07-31 asserting that reverse() keeps text
# valid, found that it did not, spent a few hours as a `@boundary-pending` probe
# recording the broken output, and was rewritten to its original intent the moment the
# fix landed. That is the whole lifecycle of a pending tripwire, start to finish.
#
# THE BUG: reverse() reversed BYTES, so every multi-byte codepoint was shredded.
# `"世界".reverse()` came back as invalid UTF-8 with codePointCount 0, from input that
# had 2. ASCII was unaffected — `"abc".reverse()` is `"cba"` either way — which is
# exactly why the corpus never noticed and why output_sweep could not have caught it:
# nothing recorded reverses non-ASCII, so there was nothing to regress from.
#
# THE FIX: `_str_reverse` in stdlib_preamble.zig walks the string codepoint by
# codepoint and copies each one's bytes intact into descending slots, so byte order
# WITHIN a codepoint is preserved while codepoint order reverses. Invalid UTF-8 input
# falls back to a byte reverse rather than panicking — reverse() has no way to report
# an error, and garbage-in-garbage-out beats aborting a user's program.
#
# Both compilers call the same helper: the bootstrap is still the regen authority and
# still builds every --gui-backend, so a selfhost-only fix would leave it live there.
def main()
    var c = "世界"
    var a = "abc"
    var e = "é"

    # ASCII is the control: it behaved correctly before the fix and must still.
    print("asciiRev=[${a.reverse()}]")
    print("asciiRevValid=${a.reverse().isValidUtf8()}")

    # The bug, now fixed: valid in, valid out, and the RIGHT text.
    var rev = c.reverse()
    print("cjkOrigValid=${c.isValidUtf8()}")
    print("cjkOrigCps=${c.codePointCount()}")
    print("cjkRevValid=${rev.isValidUtf8()}")
    print("cjkRevCps=${rev.codePointCount()}")
    print("cjkRevLen=${rev.len}")
    print("cjkRev=[${rev}]")

    # Two-byte Latin-1 corrupts the same way a 3-byte CJK codepoint did, so it is
    # checked separately — this was never a CJK-only or 3-byte-only edge.
    print("accentRevValid=${e.reverse().isValidUtf8()}")
    print("accentRevCps=${e.reverse().codePointCount()}")

    # Reversing twice is the identity, which is the property a byte reverse also
    # satisfied — included precisely so it cannot stand in as proof on its own.
    print("doubleReverse=${c.reverse().reverse() == c}")

    # Mixed ASCII and non-ASCII in one string, where a byte reverse would have put
    # the multi-byte codepoint's bytes back-to-front in the middle of valid ASCII.
    var m = "aé中b"
    print("mixedRevValid=${m.reverse().isValidUtf8()}")
    print("mixedRevCps=${m.reverse().codePointCount()}")
    print("mixedRev=[${m.reverse()}]")
